NAME
    sysctl - read and write kernel parameters

SYNOPSIS
    sysctl [OPTION...] [VARIABLE[=VALUE]]...

DESCRIPTION
    Reads or writes a runtime kernel parameter. Every parameter is a
    small file under /proc/sys, and the name it is known by is the
    path with '.' (or '/') where the path has '/': `kernel.hostname'
    is /proc/sys/kernel/hostname. With no operand, nothing is done.

    An operand with an '=' assigns a value (`sysctl -w a=b' is the
    same as `sysctl a=b'); one without reads the parameter and prints
    `name = value'. VNU carries a small /proc/sys/kernel: ostype,
    osrelease, osrevision and version are read-only facts (the same
    values uname(2) reports), and hostname and domainname may also be
    written. Changing hostname is the same as rewriting
    /etc/hostname; changing domainname rewrites /etc/domainname.

OPTIONS
    -a, --all           display every parameter in /proc/sys
    -b, --binary        print a value without its trailing newline
    -e, --ignore        ignore errors about unknown keys
    -N, --names         list only the parameter names
    -n, --values        list only the values
    -q, --quiet         do not echo `name = value' when writing
    -w, --write         the operands are name=value assignments
    -p, --load[=FILE]   read assignments from FILE
                        (/etc/sysctl.conf when none is given)
    -r, --pattern ERE   with -a, show only names matching the
                        POSIX extended regular expression ERE
    -h, --help          show this help
    -V, --version       show the version

    FILE has one `name = value' per line; blank lines and lines whose
    first non-blank character is '#' are ignored.

EXIT STATUS
    0 when every operand was handled, 1 when any read, write or load
    failed.

SEE ALSO
    uname(1), man(1)
